<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="3" to="5" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="b" from="3" to="5" step="1" exclude="$a"/>
    <curve name="ellipse" variable="t">
        ( $a*(sqrt(10)*cos(t)/5 - sqrt(6)*sin(t)/3), $b*(sqrt(10)*cos(t)/5 + sqrt(6)*sin(t)/3) )
    </curve>
    <point name="P" styleNumber="2" showCoordsWhenDragging="false">
        <constrainTo>$ellipse</constrainTo>
        <label>($P.x, $P.y)</label>
    </point>
    <number name="m">(-2/$a^2*$P.x-1/(2*$a*$b)*$P.y)/(2/$b^2*$P.y+1/(2*$a*$b)*$P.x)</number>
<!--
    <vector name="r">$P</vector>
    <vector name="rP">(-$P.y, $P.x)</vector>
  -->
</setup>
<p>
    In the figure below, there is a plot of the ellipse 
  <me>
    \frac{x^2}{<math simplify>$a^2</math>} + \frac{xy}{<math simplify>2*$a*$b</math>}+\frac{y^2}{<math simplify>$b^2</math>} = 1
  </me> 
  together with a moveable point that you can drag along the ellipse. 
</p>
<graph
    fixAxes="true" showNavigation="false"
    xmin="-6" xmax="6"
    ymin="-6" ymax="6" >
    <shortDescription>
        An ellipse with a point that can be dragged along the curve.
    </shortDescription>
    <description>
        The ellipse 
  <me>
    \frac{x^2}{$a^2} + \frac{xy}{<math simplify>2*$a*$b</math>}+\frac{y^2}{$b^2} = 1
  </me> 
  together with a moveable point that you can drag along the ellipse. 
    </description>
    $ellipse
    $P
  <!-- 
    <lineSegment 
        endpoints="$r-0.5*$rP $r+0.5*$rP"
        styleNumber="2">
    </lineSegment> 
  -->
    <label 
        anchor="(1, -5)"
        positionFromAnchor="right">Slope of tangent line = $m
    </label>

</graph>

<p>
  If we differentiate implicitly and find <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>, it turns out that 
  <me>
    \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{ -\frac{2}{<math simplify>$a^2</math>}x - \frac{1}{<math simplify>2*$a*$b</math>}y }{  \frac{1}{<math simplify>2*$a*$b</math>}x + \frac{2}{<math simplify>$b^2</math>}y}
  </me>
  We are interested in finding precise locations on the ellipse where the tangent line is either horizontal or vertical.  While we can estimate those from the graph, we need to use algebraic reasoning to achieve desired accuracy.
</p>

<p>
    <ol marker="a">
        <li>
          <p>
            To find a point on the ellipse where the tangent line is horizontal, the first condition that must hold is that <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = 0</m>.  Use the provided equation for <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> above to find an equation involving <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that must hold if the tangent line is horizontal.
          </p>
          
          <answer>
            <mathInput name="xySlope0">
                <label>The equation that must hold is</label>
            </mathInput>
            <award>
                -2/$a^2*x-1/(2*$a*$b)*y = 0
              <!-- i.e., y = -4*$b/$a x -->
            </award>
          </answer>

          <p>
            In addition to the equation you just found that makes that <m>\frac{dy}{dx} = 0</m>, for the point to lie on the ellipse, the point must also satisfy the equation of the ellipse: 
  <me>
    \frac{x^2}{$a^2} + \frac{xy}{<math simplify>2*$a*$b</math>}+\frac{y^2}{$b^2} = 1
  </me> 
            Solve your most recent equation for <m>y</m> in terms of <m>x</m>, substitute the result into the equation for the ellipse, and thus find the a value of <m>x</m> where the tangent line to the ellipse is horizontal.  Report your result to an accuracy of at least 4 decimal places.       
          </p>
          <answer allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001" allowedErrorIsAbsolute>
            <mathInput name="Slope0xValue">
                <label>An <m>x</m>-value where the tangent line is horizontal is <m>x = </m></label>
            </mathInput>
            <award>
                <when>
                  $Slope0xValue = $a/sqrt(15) or $Slope0xValue = -$a/sqrt(15)
                </when>
            </award>
          </answer>

          
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Vertical tangent line?
          </p>
        </li>

    </ol>
</p>